edwm - a customized dynamic window manager
==========================================
edwm is a fork of dwm 6.5. Windows float by default and are arranged with a
task view, half screen snapping and side by side snapping instead of
switchable layouts. Windows can also be moved and resized from the keyboard,
moved by holding both mouse buttons, and minimised to a stack in the corner.
See dwm.1 (man dwm) for the full list of key and mouse bindings.

On top of that it adds:

  * a theme system that changes colours, tone and fonts while it runs,
    with no recompile and no restart
  * a taskbar: one clickable button per window on the current screen
  * an XEmbed system tray
  * D-Bus session setup, so applications stop breaking, and an org.edwm.WM
    service for controlling the window manager


Requirements
------------
Header files for Xlib, Xft, Xinerama, XInput2, fontconfig and libpng, and
optionally libdbus.

    Debian, Ubuntu  libx11-dev libxft-dev libxinerama-dev libxi-dev \
                    libfontconfig-dev libpng-dev libdbus-1-dev
    Arch            libx11 libxft libxinerama libxi fontconfig libpng dbus
    FreeBSD         libX11 libXft libXinerama libXi fontconfig png dbus \
                    pkgconf

XInput2 is used for the two mouse button move gesture. libpng decodes tray
and menu icons and is not optional. libdbus is: comment out DBUSLIBS and
DBUSFLAGS in config.mk to build without it, and edwm still runs and is still
themable through edwmctl, but stops offering the org.edwm.WM service, the
StatusNotifierItem tray and applet menus.


Portability
-----------
Linux and the BSDs are both supported, and neither needs configuring. Library
paths come from pkg-config rather than being hardcoded, so /usr and
/usr/local both work, and the one platform-specific piece -- watching the
theme file for changes, which is inotify on Linux and kqueue on the BSDs --
is selected from the compiler's own predefined macros in dwm.c. Everything
else is POSIX. On a platform with neither mechanism the build still succeeds
and only live reload is lost; edwmctl reload and SIGHUP still work.

Both GNU make and BSD make build it, so `make` is right on every platform.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk if your setup needs it (edwm installs into /usr/local by
default), then:

    make clean install          # the window manager, edwmctl and the themes
    make install-config         # a starter ~/.config/edwm, never overwrites


Running edwm
------------
The supported way, which sets the session up properly:

    exec edwm-session

from ~/.xinitrc, or by installing edwm.desktop into /usr/share/xsessions and
picking edwm at the login screen. `exec dwm` still works, but see "D-Bus"
below for what you give up.

The status bar shows the root window name, as in stock dwm:

    while xsetroot -name "`date`"; do sleep 30; done &

~/.config/edwm/autostart.sh, if present, is run once at startup. That is the
place for a compositor, a notification daemon, a polkit agent and so on. See
autostart.sh.example.


Theming
-------
The theme lives in ~/.config/edwm/theme.conf and is re-read the moment it is
saved. See theme.conf.example, which documents every key.

A theme is a named palette, plus schemes that refer to palette entries by
name, plus a tone transform applied to the whole palette. So recolouring the
window manager is one edit rather than twenty:

    [palette]
    accent = #88C0D0        # everything that used accent follows

    [tone]
    hue        = 20         # or rotate the entire palette at once
    saturation = 1.2

Presets live in ~/.config/edwm/themes and /usr/local/share/edwm/themes.
"theme = <name>" in theme.conf loads one as a base; anything after that line
overrides it.

    edwmctl theme list
    edwmctl theme nord
    edwmctl tone hue=+20 saturation=1.15
    edwmctl tone hue=0 saturation=1 lightness=1 contrast=1 opacity=1   # reset
    edwmctl reload

A theme that fails to parse is reported with the offending line and then
ignored: the running theme stays. Changing the font size resizes the bar and
re-lays out the screen.

The [tone] opacity key needs a 32-bit visual, which is off by default. Set
useargb = 1 in config.h and run a compositor; without one an ARGB bar renders
as garbage, which is why it is opt-in.


The taskbar
-----------
The bar lists every window on the current tag, oldest first, with the focused
one highlighted, urgent ones in the urgent colour and minimised ones dimmed.

    Button1     focus and raise; on the focused window, minimise it
    Button2     toggle always-on-top
    Button3     close

They are ordinary bindings in config.h and can be changed there.


System tray
-----------
Tray applets come in two incompatible kinds and edwm handles both, side by
side at the right of the bar. Both follow the theme's systray scheme.

XEmbed applets dock an X window into the bar. That tray advertises a 32-bit
visual, which is what stops icons rendering as black boxes.

StatusNotifierItem applets instead publish themselves on the session bus and
wait for a host to display them; nm-applet on Ubuntu is one, and this is why
it appears in no plain dwm tray. edwm provides the two services they look
for -- org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher and a StatusNotifierHost -- then fetches
each item's icon and paints it into the bar itself. Icons come from the
item's own IconPixmap when it offers one, and otherwise from an icon-theme
lookup (PNG only; SVG-only themes would need librsvg). Left click sends
Activate, middle SecondaryActivate, right ContextMenu.

Requires the libdbus build. If another StatusNotifierWatcher already owns the
name -- running edwm inside a GNOME session, say -- edwm leaves it alone and
shows no SNI icons rather than fighting over it.

Right click opens the item's menu. Applets do not draw that themselves --
they publish it over DBusMenu and expect the host to render it -- so edwm
fetches the description and draws it, including submenus, separators,
disabled and hidden entries, checkmarks and the small per-entry icons.
Navigate with the mouse or with Up/Down/Left/Right, Enter and Escape.

Menus follow the theme through the menu, menusel and menudim schemes.

Not supported there: keyboard shortcut labels, icon-name lookup for menu
entries (only the inline icon-data applets send), and live updates while a
menu is open -- it is re-fetched each time it opens.

Two test clients are included, so the tray can be checked without hunting for
a real applet:

    cc -o tests/traytest tests/traytest.c -lX11
    ./tests/traytest '#ff0000'              # XEmbed
    ./tests/snitest.py audio-volume-high    # StatusNotifierItem
    ./tests/snitest.py --cycle              # ... changing icon and status


D-Bus
-----
Most "dwm has D-Bus problems" is not about the window manager speaking D-Bus.
It is that nothing told D-Bus and systemd about the session, so services
activated later start with no DISPLAY: portals hang, file choosers time out,
notifications go nowhere, keyring prompts never appear.

edwm handles that itself at startup. It finds the session bus (the one
systemd already runs, if there is one), sets XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP and friends
where they are missing, and publishes DISPLAY and XAUTHORITY into the D-Bus
and systemd --user activation environments. It deliberately never starts a
bus of its own: a second bus that the rest of the session does not know about
is the usual cause of these failures. If there is genuinely no bus it says so
and points at edwm-session, which wraps the session in dbus-run-session.

Separately, edwm serves org.edwm.WM on /org/edwm/WM:

    gdbus introspect --session --dest org.edwm.WM --object-path /org/edwm/WM

with SetTheme, GetTheme, SetTone, ReloadTheme, ListClients, FocusClient,
CloseClient, View, SetStatus and Quit, and ThemeChanged, FocusChanged,
ClientListChanged and TagsChanged signals. ListClients plus FocusChanged is
enough to drive an external window switcher without polling X.

The connection survives the bus restarting: edwm reconnects with backoff and
retakes its name.


Configuration
-------------
Colours, fonts and bar metrics belong in theme.conf and change at runtime.
Everything else -- key bindings, rules, tags, layouts -- still lives in
config.h and needs a recompile:

    make clean install

config.h and config.def.h are both tracked here and must be kept in step.
